1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,600
The Chinese Banks That Became OCBC

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:07,993
On the morning of the 20th of
November 1913, a crowd gathered

3
00:00:08,073 --> 00:00:12,319
outside the Kwong Yik Bank on
Kling Street to find a notice

4
00:00:12,399 --> 00:00:16,725
pinned to its doors: the bank
could not pay its depositors.

5
00:00:16,725 --> 00:00:21,125
Singapore's first Chinese
bank had lasted ten years.

6
00:00:21,125 --> 00:00:26,038
Over the next two decades, more
Chinese banks opened, most of them

7
00:00:26,118 --> 00:00:30,879
run by and for one dialect
community, until the Great Depression

8
00:00:30,959 --> 00:00:35,725
pushed three of them into the
merger that created OCBC in 1932.

9
00:00:35,725 --> 00:00:38,668
When the European banks arrived from 1840,

10
00:00:38,748 --> 00:00:43,347
most of their Chinese customers
reached them through a comprador,

11
00:00:43,427 --> 00:00:46,450
a local intermediary who vouched for them.

12
00:00:46,450 --> 00:00:50,038
By the early 1900s, Singapore's
Chinese merchants were

13
00:00:50,118 --> 00:00:53,434
running rubber estates,
shipping lines and trading

14
00:00:53,514 --> 00:00:57,170
houses of their own, and some
of them wanted banks that

15
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:00,850
would lend to their own
community on their own terms.

16
00:01:00,850 --> 00:01:03,708
The first was the Kwong Yik Bank,

17
00:01:03,788 --> 00:01:07,350
which opened at 26 Kling Street in 1903.

18
00:01:07,350 --> 00:01:11,567
It was founded by Cantonese
businessmen led by Wong-Ah-Fook,

19
00:01:11,647 --> 00:01:16,939
a building contractor and gambier
planter, and it offered ordinary banking,

20
00:01:17,019 --> 00:01:20,600
mortgages and loans to a
mostly Chinese clientele.

21
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:24,213
In November 1913, after weeks of rumours,

22
00:01:24,293 --> 00:01:26,725
depositors ran on the bank.

23
00:01:26,725 --> 00:01:31,576
According to the Singapore Free
Press, its funds were overdrawn

24
00:01:31,656 --> 00:01:36,351
by about $560,000 when the
directors decided to stop payment,

25
00:01:36,431 --> 00:01:41,675
and a notice was posted on the
doors early on the 20th of November.

26
00:01:41,675 --> 00:01:45,331
A crowd gathered outside,
most of them, the paper said,

27
00:01:45,411 --> 00:01:48,659
from the poorer members
of the Chinese community,

28
00:01:48,739 --> 00:01:52,475
and a special body of police
had to guard the entrance.

29
00:01:52,475 --> 00:01:56,636
The bank went into liquidation,
and its liquidators exposed

30
00:01:56,716 --> 00:02:01,675
irregular record-keeping and financial
malpractice in its management.

31
00:02:01,675 --> 00:02:06,644
In 1915 the colony amended its
Companies Bill to restrict the

32
00:02:06,724 --> 00:02:11,525
loans a bank could make to its
own directors and officers.

33
00:02:11,525 --> 00:02:14,775
The next banks followed dialect lines.

34
00:02:14,775 --> 00:02:18,584
The Teochew community's
Sze Hai Tong Banking and

35
00:02:18,664 --> 00:02:22,150
Insurance Company was
established in January

36
00:02:22,230 --> 00:02:26,200
1907 on Kling Street,
staffed mainly by Teochews.

37
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:31,009
The Hokkien community's first
bank, the Chinese Commercial Bank,

38
00:02:31,089 --> 00:02:36,281
opened on the 2nd of December 1912
with $2 million in issued capital,

39
00:02:36,361 --> 00:02:41,250
and is credited with popularising
cheques among Chinese traders.

40
00:02:41,250 --> 00:02:46,640
Its first chairman was Lee-Choon-Guan
and its vice-chairman Lim-Boon-Keng,

41
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:49,966
and its managing director
was Lim-Peng-Siang,

42
00:02:50,046 --> 00:02:54,925
who went on to found a second
Hokkien bank, Ho Hong Bank, in 1917.

43
00:02:54,925 --> 00:02:59,277
Each bank drew its directors,
staff and depositors mostly from

44
00:02:59,357 --> 00:03:04,075
its own community, and when trouble
came, that community mattered.

45
00:03:04,075 --> 00:03:08,727
On the 4th of August 1914,
when war broke out in Europe,

46
00:03:08,807 --> 00:03:12,698
a panicking public drew
about $260,000 from the

47
00:03:12,778 --> 00:03:17,425
Chinese Commercial Bank, and
it had to suspend payment.

48
00:03:17,425 --> 00:03:21,766
The government examined its
accounts, declared that its assets

49
00:03:21,846 --> 00:03:26,125
exceeded its liabilities, and
helped it reopen in September.

50
00:03:26,125 --> 00:03:30,470
According to Song Ong Siang,
the Teochew business community

51
00:03:30,550 --> 00:03:33,845
stood by Sze Hai Tong
through the same panic,

52
00:03:33,925 --> 00:03:39,175
and its largest shareholder,
Low-Peng-Yam, put $500,000 into the bank.

53
00:03:39,175 --> 00:03:44,275
The boom after the First World War
brought the third Hokkien bank.

54
00:03:44,275 --> 00:03:49,268
The Oversea-Chinese Bank was
formed in 1919 with $5.25 million

55
00:03:49,348 --> 00:03:55,813
in paid-up capital, backed by the rubber
magnates Lim-Nee-Soon and Tan-Ean-Kiam,

56
00:03:55,893 --> 00:03:59,575
and its fortunes were
tied closely to rubber.

57
00:03:59,575 --> 00:04:02,318
Lim-Boon-Keng persuaded Oei-Tiong-Ham,

58
00:04:02,398 --> 00:04:06,553
the wealthy Hokkien industrialist
of Java, to buy shares,

59
00:04:06,633 --> 00:04:10,125
and Chinese merchants in
Rangoon backed it too.

60
00:04:10,125 --> 00:04:13,954
It opened branches in Penang,
Rangoon, Kuala Lumpur,

61
00:04:14,034 --> 00:04:17,787
Malacca, Amoy and Sumatra,
and much of its business

62
00:04:17,867 --> 00:04:21,250
was remittances from
Southeast Asia to China.

63
00:04:21,250 --> 00:04:25,000
The Cantonese community
tried again as well.

64
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,227
In March 1920 Eu-Tong-Sen,
the tin and medicine magnate,

65
00:04:29,307 --> 00:04:34,380
opened the Lee Wah Bank on South
Canal Road to serve the Cantonese,

66
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:38,075
and its staff were expected
to speak Cantonese.

67
00:04:38,075 --> 00:04:40,821
The Depression hit the Chinese banks hard,

68
00:04:40,901 --> 00:04:45,275
because many of their customers
were rubber planters and dealers.

69
00:04:45,275 --> 00:04:49,316
In 1932 the Oversea-Chinese
Bank's Rangoon branch suffered

70
00:04:49,396 --> 00:04:52,939
a run after local Chinese
leaders fell out with the

71
00:04:53,019 --> 00:04:56,491
bank over its remittance
service, and although the

72
00:04:56,571 --> 00:05:00,550
bank survived it, it could
not ride out the slump alone.

73
00:05:00,550 --> 00:05:04,845
Its directors opened confidential
talks with Ho Hong Bank,

74
00:05:04,925 --> 00:05:09,225
and the Chinese Commercial
Bank was then invited to join.

75
00:05:09,225 --> 00:05:11,931
On the 30th of August 1932 the

76
00:05:12,011 --> 00:05:15,075
Straits Times printed the scheme.

77
00:05:15,075 --> 00:05:18,833
The directors' letter to
shareholders listed its advantages,

78
00:05:18,913 --> 00:05:22,926
beginning with "Union is Strength":
the merged bank would have a

79
00:05:23,006 --> 00:05:27,083
stronger financial position,
overlapping offices could be closed,

80
00:05:27,163 --> 00:05:31,432
and "unnecessary competition amongst
the three banks in those places

81
00:05:31,512 --> 00:05:35,350
in which they are now serving
the same community" would end.

82
00:05:35,350 --> 00:05:38,430
One share in the Chinese
Commercial Bank was

83
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:42,100
valued at three shares in
either of the other two.

84
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:46,220
The Oversea-Chinese Banking
Corporation was incorporated

85
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:51,095
on the 31st of October 1932, with
$10 million in paid-up capital,

86
00:05:51,175 --> 00:05:54,395
and opened for business
at the start of 1933

87
00:05:54,475 --> 00:05:58,970
in the China Building on Chulia
Street, the old Kling Street,

88
00:05:59,050 --> 00:06:02,800
where Kwong Yik had failed
nineteen years earlier.

89
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:07,554
The talks that created it were
led by Tan-Ean-Kiam of the

90
00:06:07,634 --> 00:06:13,575
Oversea-Chinese Bank and Lee-Kong-Chian
of the Chinese Commercial Bank,

91
00:06:13,655 --> 00:06:17,725
and Lee went on to chair
OCBC from 1938 to 1964.

92
00:06:17,725 --> 00:06:22,075
The merger joined the three
Hokkien banks, but not the others.

93
00:06:22,075 --> 00:06:26,950
The Teochew bank kept its
independence for another four decades.

94
00:06:26,950 --> 00:06:32,203
Renamed Four Seas
Communications Bank in 1964,

95
00:06:32,283 --> 00:06:36,225
it was taken over by OCBC in 1972.

96
00:06:36,225 --> 00:06:39,058
The Cantonese Lee Wah Bank

97
00:06:39,138 --> 00:06:42,275
was acquired by UOB in 1973.

98
00:06:42,275 --> 00:06:46,097
Where it fits in the bigger
story: OCBC is usually

99
00:06:46,177 --> 00:06:50,000
remembered as one of
Singapore's big local banks.

100
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,752
It began as three banks that
belonged to one community,

101
00:06:53,832 --> 00:06:59,116
formed after a decade in which Chinese
banks were founded along dialect lines

102
00:06:59,196 --> 00:07:03,725
and the first of them failed in
a single morning on Kling Street.
